2. Dictionary data (data/kanji.json)
   Derived from KANJIDIC2 and JMdict, the property of the Electronic Dictionary
   Research and Development Group (EDRDG), used in conformance with the Group's
   licence: Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International
   (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/).
   https://www.edrdg.org/edrdg/licence.html

3. Stroke-order data (data/strokes/*.json)
   Derived from KanjiVG, Copyright (C) 2009-2026 Ulrich Apel and contributors,
   Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0
   (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/). https://kanjivg.tagaini.net

4. Example sentences (data/ex/*.json)
   Most are from the Tanaka corpus of Japanese–English example sentences
   (Tatoeba project, also distributed with JMdict), © Tanaka Yasuhito and
   Tatoeba contributors, Creative Commons Attribution 2.0 France
   (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/fr/). https://tatoeba.org
   Readings were taken from the corpus B-lines where indexed, otherwise from
   JMdict headwords (EDRDG, CC BY-SA 4.0). A sentence tagged "g" in the file
   was written for this site because no usable corpus sentence was found; those
   drafts are also stored here so they can be reviewed.

5. Components (data/comp/, data/part/)
   Structure, positions and stroke groups from KanjiVG (CC BY-SA 3.0).
   Which kanji contain a component also uses KRADFILE and RADKFILE,
   © Michael Raine, James Breen and EDRDG (CC BY-SA).
   Radical names, meanings, and phonetic (sound) vs semantic (meaning)
   labels are from Kanjium by mifunetoshiro (CC BY-SA 4.0),
   https://github.com/mifunetoshiro/kanjium — itself based on KANJIDIC
   and EDRDG data. A part is marked “sound” or “meaning” only when
   Kanjium records the kanji as a phono-semantic compound.
   Common words on a part page are from JMdict (EDRDG, CC BY-SA 4.0).

6. Handwriting reference (data/hw.bin)
   Stroke shapes derived from KanjiVG, same CC BY-SA 3.0 licence as the
   stroke-order files. The recognizer in hw.js is original code. Its
   stroke-order-free matching follows KanjiCanvas by Dominik Klein
   (MIT, https://github.com/asdfjkl/kanjicanvas). KanjiCanvas is not
   copied into this repository; the link above is the attribution.

The modified dictionary and stroke files in data/ are distributed under the
same licences as their sources (CC BY-SA). Example sentences follow CC BY 2.0 FR.
data/hw.bin follows CC BY-SA 3.0.
